#
This is a short document to descibe the interfaces for the new additive 
noise written in python for both the WRF and MPAS WoFS systems.


# grid_refl_obs.py
 
python grid_refl_obs.py wrf_data/obs_seq.nc.202303312100 wrf_data/wrfout_d01_2023-03-31_21:00:00_1 154221 75600 wrf


      The input for this command is not changed, except ADDING a model type to the end of the command ("wrf" or "mpas")

      a) The 1st argument is the netCDF obs_seq file
      b) The 2nd argument is the minimum reflectivity for adding noise
      b) The 3rd argument is the minimum innovation for adding noise
      b) The 4th argument is the netCDF wrf file
      c) The 5th argument is the number of Julian seconds
      d) The 6th argument is the number of Julian days
      e) The 7th argument is the model type:  wrf / mpas


# add_pert_where_high_refl.py
#
python add_pert_where_high_refl.py wrf_data/wrfinput_d01 9000.0 3000.0 0.50 0.50 0.0 0.50 0.50 0.0 154221 75600 35 wrf

      The input for this command HAS changed slightly.  The input refl_*.txt file is no longer needed.  
      And a model type is the last argument at the end of the command

      Important:  WRF file will be overwritten!

      a) The 1st argument is the netCDF wrf file - this will be OVER WRITTEN!
      b) The 2nd argument is the horizontal smoothing scale in meters (usually ~ 9000 m)
      b) The 3rd argument is the vertical smoothing scale in meters (usually ~ 3000 m)
      c) The 4th-9th arguments are the standard deviations of the noise for the U/V/W/THETA/Td/QV fields
      c) The 10th argument is the number of Julian seconds
      d) The 11th argument is the number of Julian days
      e) The 12th argument is the model type:  wrf / mpas

Note, that for MPAS, specify the same noise level for U & V 


PYTHON ENV:

Included in the directory is a python environment yaml file.  I highly, highly recommend, if you are creating
your own env, to install micromamba and use that to manage your python envs.
You can do this on the command line without root privileges. I think Vecna has it installed (or ask Brett).
We use micromamba on our cloud systems.

after installing micromamba, you can create the environment:

      minimamba create -n wofs_an wofs_an.yaml

then activate it

      minimamba activate wofs_an


-->> included in this environment are the NCO operators.
